# Line endings.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: several suites in tests/ read repo source files off disk
# and assert on multi-line string literals written with `\n` — for example
# tests/addon-purchase.test.ts:
#
#     expect(stripe).toContain('status: "pending",\n            purchaseKind,');
#
# The assertion is correct and CI (Linux, LF) agrees with it. On a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true git rewrote every file to CRLF on the way
# out, so the same assertion compared CRLF file content against an LF
# expectation and failed for a reason that had nothing to do with the code.
# Three such assertions failed on every Windows worktree while CI stayed green.
#
# `eol=lf` fixes it at the source — the working tree gets LF regardless of the
# developer's core.autocrlf setting — rather than by weakening the assertions.
#
# BLAST RADIUS: this changes the working-tree line endings of every text file
# for every developer whose core.autocrlf is true or input-with-crlf. Editors in
# use here (VS Code, the JetBrains family, vim) all handle LF natively on
# Windows. Nothing in this repo is consumed by a CRLF-only tool: there are no
# .bat/.cmd files, and the two tracked .sh files plus the .husky hooks require
# LF anyway. Committed content does not change — git already stored LF for every
# tracked text file bar one (see the renormalise commit).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch interpreters require CRLF. None are tracked today; this keeps a
# future one correct rather than silently broken by the blanket rule above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary. `text=auto` already detects these by content; declaring them is belt
# and braces so a future file that happens to lack a NUL byte early cannot be
# mangled by end-of-line conversion.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.eot  binary
